On Nov 26, 9:33 pm, Rachel Elizabeth Dillon <rac...@akrasiac.org>
wrote:
> I'd disagree that roguelikes are all about dying. I play roguelikes
> to win.  I know that dying is about 500 of the steps on the path to
> winning, but my goal is not to get a high score, my goal is to win.
> I don't want victory to be a foregone conclusion, but if it's not
> attainable, I'm unlikely to stay that interested.

Rogue took this to just about the ultimate limits.  It's winnable, but
just barely.  Nethack, to a player who really knows what he's doing,
is much easier.  If you haven't bathed in spoilers however it's much
harder.

> Score is interesting to me as a means of gauging progress forward in the
> game, and competing with other people who are learning the game at the
> same time as I am. Once I've won I don't really care, at least for any
> roguelike I've won (which I guess is only three: Nethack, Crawl, DoomRL).

This is a valid way of approaching the game, and I don't suggest
winning should be removed.  But it should be remembered that the
player gets a score even when he wins, and community play (which,
after a long fallow period, has reemerged as a vital way to experience
Nethack) finds score very useful.

> NetHack is way more of a puzzle game. The identification game is much
> richer and there is just enough plot that new players try to #chat with
> everything (and are, in my experience, disappointed when it largely
> doesn't work). In Gehennom and on, though, vanilla NetHack is basically
> a dungeon slog like Crawl is, and it is really unimpressive there,
> in my opinion.

But Crawl is a very interesting dungeon slog, far more interesting
than Genhennom certainly.  Its sub-dungeons are an object lesson on
how to make dungeon branches distinct from each other.

> I think Spork fixes some of that, but maybe not enough.

The problem is that Genhennom really needs to be a major part of the
game to live up to the legacy of Hell, but the player is a tank by
that part, and mazes are relatively uninteresting to explore here.
One place to start is to beef up the demonic opposition in the game.
Another is to make Genhennom levels mazes with rooms interspersed, and
add terrain features.  Another possibility is to shorten it, but
that's taking the easy way out.

> NetHack's food is much more fun to me than Crawl's food. NetHack's food is
> more than just a timer; Crawl's food is something I have to pay attention
> to or else I will be in an emergency, and nothing more.

Is there reason to believe it could not be both?

> NetHack has lore. Everyone has heard of it, and it's easy to find.
> A lot of good games get largely ignored due to lack of advertising.

> The guiding principle around NetHack's construction _was_ to be
> something akin to a 1st edition D&D simulator. I should hope it's moved
> on since then.  Which is not to say that I don't agree with you about
> restore ability, just that I should hope NetHack has moved on. D&D
> simulator has been done a lot of times now.

I really don't think it has moved on.  And actually, a real D&D
simulator has almost never been seen except in the roguelike sphere.
All the D&D licensed games I'm aware of, going back to and including
the Gold Box line, still have far less dynamic game worlds than either
roguelikes or pen-and-paper D&D.

And dammit, a D&D simulator isn't a bad thing!  Nethack may stick too
closely to its item and monster archetypes, but it's a great place to
start from.  IMO, of course.

